PARTIALLY ROOT-CAUSED AND FIXED 2026-09-04 (seat14). Original defect (captures nothing at all, [][][] for every I) was: the capture-target lowering for a deferred SUBSCRIPT (*A<I>) never routed through want-name mode, so the compiled thunk evaluated A<I> to its VALUE and the runtime's SN4-CAP-NAME-STRICT check correctly (but silently, since nothing branches on match failure) refused the assignment as "not a name." Fixed in lower_snobol4.c: TT_IDX capture targets now collect via sno_expr_collect_nm (matching TT_INDIRECT's existing treatment) and a new sx_idx_container() helper lowers the subscript's container without the trailing IR_DEREF that TT_IDX's ordinary lowering always adds -- verified via a minimal repro (single I, single match) now capturing correctly in both m3 and m4. REMAINING, NEWLY-FOUND DEFECT on this exact witness: this program's shape is a match-AND-REPLACE loop (`S PAT =`, run three times over the same S with I=1,2,3) -- on the FIRST iteration the replace consumes the ENTIRE remaining subject (S becomes '' instead of 'yz') rather than just the matched LEN(1) span, so iterations 2 and 3 then correctly fail to match anything against the now-empty S and never reach the capture step at all. Isolated: a plain (non-deferred) capture target with the identical LEN(1)-match-and-replace-three-times shape replaces correctly each time (verified against both scrip and the live oracle); swapping in a deferred SUBSCRIPT target is what triggers the over-wide replace, specifically on the iteration where the capture-queue's mid-flight call into the compiled "EXPRNM$N" thunk (via rt_call_proc_descr, inside rt_dcap_pump) runs between bb_match_end.cpp's cursor banking and bb_match_replace.cpp's read of it -- root cause not bottomed out past that (asm-diff and a register-level gdb trace across that call boundary is the next step; RULES.md ASM-DIFF-FIRST order). Current result: [x][][] (first slot right, second and third still empty). Filed as its own finding rather than